Transient benign palpitations in an anxious patient temporally associated with heavy caffeine intake
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Subjective rapid heartbeat awareness, Caffeine consumption history, Spontaneous resolution

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-042
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of rapid heartbeat and chest fluttering requiring urgent assessment to exclude cardiac arrhythmia, ischemia, or structural heart disease.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified sudden palpitations and a sensation of a racing heart. Urgent clinical evaluation was strictly required prior to the next in-hours period to assess hemodynamic stability, auscultate the heart, and confidently rule out dangerous tachyarrhythmias (such as atrial fibrillation or SVT) which could lead to acute decompensation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My heart is racing and fluttering in my chest, and I feel incredibly jittery.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes an intermittent subjective awareness of a rapid or forceful heartbeat starting an hour ago. Reports a coinciding feeling of mild jitteriness or inner tension. Admits to a clear temporal association with excessive consumption of highly caffeinated energy drinks today. Notes spontaneous resolution of the peak rapid rhythm as the stimulant effect slowly wanes. Complete absence of associated syncope, near-syncope, or crushing chest pain. Denies shortness of breath at rest.
Functional Status: Fully independent, currently resting anxiously on the couch.
Social/Safety Concerns: Anxiety is compounding the sympathetic response.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/85 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, appearing mildly anxious but in no acute respiratory distress.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular examination reveals a normal baseline resting sinus rhythm with no ectopy noted during the exam. Clear heart sounds (S1, S2) without murmurs, rubs, or gallops. Diagnostic triad confirmed: subjective rapid heartbeat awareness, caffeine consumption history, and spontaneous resolution towards a normal rate. Intact peripheral pulses without deficit or irregularity. Clear lung fields. No peripheral edema or jugular venous distension.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty large energy drink cans on the desk.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient not taking regular stimulants or thyroid medication.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Transient benign palpitations in an anxious patient temporally associated with heavy caffeine intake - Status: Improving
Differentials:

Supraventricular Tachycardia (SVT) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Current regular sinus rhythm under 100 bpm, gradual onset/offset linked to stimulant intake, and lack of hemodynamic compromise.

Atrial Fibrillation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Regular, strong peripheral pulses and a regular rhythm on auscultation.

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of typical angina, pressure-like chest pain, or radiating discomfort.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden onset of symptomatic palpitations can be the first sign of a life-threatening arrhythmia or acute ischemic event. Delaying a physical assessment until regular clinic hours posed an unacceptable risk of missing a sustained tachyarrhythmia requiring emergent cardioversion. The urgent home evaluation confirmed hemodynamic stability, verified a regular sinus rhythm, and identified a clear chemical trigger, successfully averting an unnecessary emergency department visit and managing the patient safely in the community.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance regarding the structurally normal heart exam. Advice to aggressively orally hydrate to aid clearance of the caffeine. Planned non-urgent 12-lead ECG with the regular GP for baseline documentation if palpitations recur.
Positive Behavioral Change: Limit caffeine intake strictly to one standard cup of coffee per day consumed before 10 AM, removing all access to concentrated energy drinks from the home environment to prevent future episodes.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1 week for a baseline ECG.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you experience severe, heavy chest pain, feel like you are about to faint, develop severe shortness of breath, or if your pulse races uncontrollably and irregularly.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical based on current stable exam; undocumented arrhythmias require formal ECG capture during an episode.
Clinical Course: The jitteriness and palpitations will resolve entirely over the next 4 to 6 hours as the caffeine is metabolized.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you pass out, if the fluttering in your chest becomes constant and very fast, or if you feel pain spreading to your jaw or left arm.

PATIENT EDUCATION

